## Phoenix fixes evaluator requests for Anthropic prompts with extended thinking

Source: Arize Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/arize-phoenix-v21.2.0
Published: 2026-11-18
Channel: github

Arize Phoenix is an open-source platform for observing and evaluating AI applications. Its server-side language model evaluators use a model to assess outputs. The maintainers found that an Anthropic evaluator request could fail when its prompt enabled extended thinking: the evaluator forced a tool call, a combination the API rejected with a request error.

The fix checks the invocation parameters before sending the evaluation request. When extended thinking is enabled, Phoenix now allows automatic tool choice and adds a system instruction naming the tool, instead of forcing the tool call. A thinking setting inside the extra request body takes precedence because that body is merged last. Adaptive or disabled thinking continues to use forced tool choice, which the maintainers say the API accepts.

The change applies to the evaluator path; the playground still sends the user's chosen tool setting. The linked change reports live API checks of affected and unaffected configurations, but does not quantify how often users encountered the failure.

Why it matters: Teams running Anthropic-based Phoenix evaluators can check whether extended-thinking configurations that previously failed now complete.

